[32m+[m[32m# The git directory, when the checkout is a linked WORKTREE and therefore does not[m
[32m+[m[32m# contain one.[m
[32m+[m[32m#[m
[32m+[m[32m# A worktree's `.git` is a file reading `gitdir: <abs path>`, and that path lives[m
[32m+[m[32m# under the primary checkout's `.git`, outside the repo bind. Without it every[m
[32m+[m[32m# `git` call inside the sandbox fails: a suite that reads HEAD saw a directory that[m
[32m+[m[32m# is not a repository, and one that reads a committed blob saw exit 128, both from a[m
[32m+[m[32m# tree that is a perfectly good checkout on the host. Binding the primary `.git` at[m
[32m+[m[32m# its own host path is what makes the pointer resolve, since the pointer is absolute[m
[32m+[m[32m# and `commondir` is relative to it.[m
[32m+[m[32m#[m
[32m+[m[32m# Read-only, and skipped entirely when that path is inside the home being hidden:[m
[32m+[m[32m# the whole contract of this sandbox is that the home is unreachable, and no[m
[32m+[m[32m# convenience is worth handing part of it back. A git-dependent suite then behaves[m
[32m+[m[32m# as it did before this bind existed.[m
[32m+[m[32mSANDBOX_GITDIR=""[m
